what My own 20260917.023717 paper named two falsifiers for its central mechanism claim -- that the 14.4x gap between reading 16.9 MB as 527 files and as one tar is a per-file device round trip, since readahead cannot cross an open. Falsifier one: a queue-depth-1 4 KiB read latency far from the 200-350 microseconds that claim needs kills the mechanism. Falsifier two: the 14x gap surviving on real NVMe kills the reading outright. Neither was run and the paper says so on its face. Build a QD1 single-block read latency probe in Rye on top of the existing page_evict eviction, measure this device's real per-operation latency, and set it beside the 279 microsecond per-file marginal cost the paper measured. Report the concrete fact at the door first: /dev/vda reports ROTA=1, so the kernel believes this disk spins, which sets readahead size and the I/O scheduler -- a fact that changes what every storage number this lane has published means. No NVMe stands on this pier, so falsifier two is named as unreachable here rather than answered. Prove the probe from both sides in a pen.
